      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day, which is double the maximum labeled metformin dose. The maximum is 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release, and usually 2,000 mg/day for extended-release. A dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, even with normal renal function.\n\nCould you check what was intended? Some possibilities:\n- **850 mg three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day and the usual maximum.\n- **1000 mg twice daily with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **500 mg three times daily**, or another dose, if the patient is starting therapy. Metformin is usually started low (500 mg once or twice daily) and titrated up.\n\nTell me the corrected dose and frequency and I'll enter it right away. If 1700 mg three times daily is really what you want, for example because the patient is on a special protocol, please confirm that explicitly. I'd also suggest checking with pharmacy before it goes in."
      }
