     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for a dose twice the maximum. At 5,100 mg/day, the main danger is metformin-associated lactic acidosis. It can be fatal, and normal kidney function doesn't prevent it at this dose. I can't think of an indication that would call for it.\n\nHere is what I can do right away:\n- **Enter a maximum-dose regimen**, such as 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). If the patient is new to metformin, I can enter a lower starting dose with a titration plan in the note.\n- **Enter the order with a note** asking for pharmacy review. This only makes sense if the dose is within the labeled range.\n- **Escalate to pharmacy or the attending.** If there is a protocol or specialist recommendation behind this dose, they can verify it and document it. The order can then be entered through that process.\n\nIf the goal is to have everything done before rounds, I can enter the 850 mg three times daily order now. You can then adjust it after rounds if needed. Do you want me to do that?"
      }
     ],
